      "claim": "In classical statistics, maximum likelihood estimation (MLE) is justified when the sample size $n$ is not much larger than the dimension $d$ of the estimated parameters (i.e., $n \\gg d$).",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'in classical statistics, this is a maximum rapid estimate. So this is just point estimate. While on Bayesian train work, the result of our inference is a distribution of stereo distribution.' [00:40:46\u201300:41:49] ... 'And in particular, you will apply marks-on-work with estimation in machine learning models, when n is not much larger than d, what do we get in practice? So actually, every time when you train some algorithm, you tune parameters of the model, and from statistical point of view, you perform, in most cases, you perform marks-on-work with estimations, according to your observations as a training dataset.' [00:42:47\u201300:43:19] ... 'And in fact, it's justified when n is not much larger than one, but is much larger than d, where d is a dimension of estimated parameters.' [00:41:49\u201300:42:46] This seems to be a bit confusing with the phrasing, but the key point is that the ASR states that MLE is justified when n is much larger than d (n >> d), not when n is not much larger than d. The claim says 'when n is not much larger than d', which is the opposite of what the ASR says. So the claim is not supported by the ASR. The ASR says MLE is justified when n is much larger than d (n >> d), not when n is not much larger than d. So the claim is incorrect based on the ASR."
    },
    {
      "claim": "When $n$ is not much larger than $d$, MLE can only be treated as a reasonable approximation without theoretical guarantees.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'And in particular, you will apply marks-on-work with estimation in machine learning models, when n is not much larger than d, what do we get in practice? So actually, every time when you train some algorithm, you tune parameters of the model, and from statistical point of view, you perform, in most cases, you perform marks-on-work with estimations, according to your observations as a training dataset.' [00:42:47\u201300:43:19] ... 'And in fact, it's justified when n is not much larger than one, but is much larger than d, where d is a dimension of estimated parameters.' [00:41:49\u201300:42:46] ... 'The problem is that in those cases, Microsoft estimation can only be treated as a reasonable characteristic with no theoretical guarantees.' [00:41:49\u201300:42:46] This seems to be a bit confusing with the phrasing, but the key point is that the ASR states that when n is not much larger than d (i.e., n is comparable to d), MLE can only be treated as a reasonable approximation without theoretical guarantees. So the claim is supported by the ASR."
    },
    {
      "claim": "In machine learning, when the number of observations $n$ is smaller or comparable with the number of parameters $d$, you immediately observe overfitting (gap between train and validation error).",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'Like survived estimation, under situations when n is smaller or comparable with d, you immediately observe the gap between your train error and your validation error.' [00:43:19\u201300:43:49] This directly supports the claim that when n is smaller or comparable with d, you immediately observe overfitting (gap between train and validation error)."
    },
    {
      "claim": "When $n \\gg d$, there is no gap between train and validation error (good generalization).",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'And if you apply a machine learning algorithm whose number of parameters is much smaller than the number of observations, you observe no gap.' [00:43:19\u201300:43:49] This directly supports the claim that when n is much larger than d (n >> d), there is no gap between train and validation error (good generalization)."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Deep neural networks (15-20 years ago) had small parameter counts and large datasets, so they did not overfit (MLE was optimal).",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'And this was firstly observed when first deep neural networks have appeared in the end of notice. So about 15 years ago, maybe 20 years ago. And there was a series of papers which stated that deep neural networks do not follow fit. Why so? Because the first deep neural networks were sufficiently small and they were trained on already huge data sets.' [00:43:50\u201300:44:19] This directly supports the claim that deep neural networks (15-20 years ago) had small parameter counts and large datasets, so they did not overfit (MLE was optimal)."
    },
    {
      "claim": "As deep neural networks grew larger, the condition $n \\gg d$ was violated, leading to overfitting (gap between train and validation error).",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'Later, however, the humanity designed larger deep neural networks, and this condition was violated.' [00:44:52\u201300:45:20] ... 'And immediately, when it was violated, there appeared a gap between training error and validation error.' [00:44:52\u201300:45:20] This directly supports the claim that as deep neural networks grew larger, the condition n >> d was violated, leading to overfitting (gap between train and validation error)."
    },
    {
      "claim": "In the Bayesian framework, when $n = 0$, the posterior distribution equals the prior distribution.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'And if we have no observations, of course posterior will be simply equal to prior.' [00:45:58\u201300:46:28] This directly supports the claim that when n = 0, the posterior distribution equals the prior distribution."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Bayesian updating works for any number of observations, including $n = 0$.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'Bayesian framework is applicable for any number of observations. So we can even apply it when n equals to zero.' [00:45:20\u201300:45:57] This directly supports the claim that Bayesian updating works for any number of observations, including n = 0."
    }
  ],
  "clarity_1_to_5": 4,
  "caveat": "The ASR has some confusing phrasing (e.g., 'maximum rapid estimate' vs 'maximum likelihood estimation', 'marks-on-work' vs 'maximum likelihood estimation', 'survived estimation' vs 'maximum likelihood estimation'). The claim about MLE being justified when n is not much larger than d is actually the opposite of what the ASR says (the ASR says MLE is justified when n is much larger than d)."
}
